<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_filter.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-filter">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-filter">
  <primary>FILTER</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>FILTER</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>FILTER</refname>
  <refpurpose>keep only the rows that satisfy a condition</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
FILTER [ WHERE ] <replaceable class="parameter">condition</replaceable>
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-filter-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>FILTER</command> discards every row whose condition is not true. It changes
   nothing else. The columns in scope before it are exactly the columns in scope after it.
  </para>

  <para>
   The <literal>WHERE</literal> keyword is optional and means nothing on its own.
   <literal>FILTER x &gt; 1</literal> and <literal>FILTER WHERE x &gt; 1</literal> are the
   same clause.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>FILTER</command> applies to whatever the clause before it produced, including the
   output of an aggregating <command>WITH</command>. That makes it the Cypher equivalent of
   SQL's <literal>HAVING</literal>. Two of them in a row apply in order, and the second sees
   only the rows the first kept. A row whose condition evaluates to null is discarded, as it
   would be in SQL.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>FILTER</command> is a middle clause. It cannot open a query and it cannot end one.
   The single exception is after <xref linkend="cypher-next"/>, where it may come first
   because it acts on the table carried across the boundary.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-filter-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">condition</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Any expression over the variables in scope that evaluates to
      <type>boolean</type>. This includes a subquery value expression such as
      <literal>EXISTS { }</literal> or <literal>COUNT { }</literal>.
     </para>
     <para>
      The condition has to be a boolean. A bare number is rejected with
      <literal>argument of FILTER must be type boolean, not type integer</literal>, and a bare
      property with <literal>cannot cast jsonb to boolean</literal>. Compare explicitly
      instead, or add an explicit cast.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-filter-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Keep the films from 2014 onwards:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie)
FILTER m.year &gt;= 2014
RETURN m.title AS film, m.year AS year ORDER BY year;

            film            | year
----------------------------+------
 "Interstellar"             | 2014
 "Blade Runner 2049"        | 2017
 "The Matrix Resurrections" | 2021
(3 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A <literal>WHERE</literal> attached to the pattern is the same test and produces the same
   plan. Use <command>FILTER</command> when the condition belongs to the pipeline rather than
   to one pattern.
  </para>

  <para>
   Test an aggregate. <literal>WHERE</literal> cannot follow an aggregating
   <command>RETURN</command>, and attaching it to a <command>WITH</command> means restating
   the projection. <command>FILTER</command> tests the aggregate directly:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person)-[:acted_in]-&gt;(m:movie)
WITH p.name AS actor, count(m) AS films
FILTER films &gt;= 3
RETURN actor, films ORDER BY actor;

          actor          | films
-------------------------+-------
 "Arnold Schwarzenegger" | 3
 "Keanu Reeves"          | 4
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Narrow the rows in two stages. The second <command>FILTER</command> sees only what the
   first one kept:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie)
FILTER m.genre = 'Sci-Fi'
FILTER m.year &lt; 1998
RETURN m.title AS film ORDER BY film;

        film
---------------------
 "Blade Runner"
 "Gattaca"
 "The Fifth Element"
 "Total Recall"
(4 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A <command>FILTER</command> after a write runs the write first, then decides which rows
   flow on. Here <command>SET</command> updates the film and <command>FILTER</command> decides
   whether the row is reported:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'The Matrix'})
SET m.reviewed = true
FILTER m.year = 1999
RETURN m.title AS film, m.reviewed AS reviewed;

     film     | reviewed
--------------+----------
 "The Matrix" | true
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-filter-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   Because <command>FILTER</command> cannot open a query, a statement that starts with it is a
   syntax error rather than a placement error:
<programlisting>
FILTER true;

ERROR:  syntax error at or near "FILTER"
LINE 1: FILTER true;
        ^
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Ending a query on <command>FILTER</command> raises
   <literal>Cypher query must end with RETURN, FINISH or update clause</literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>filter</literal> is an unreserved keyword, so it stays available as a variable, a
   label or a property name.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-filter-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   <command>FILTER</command> implements the GQL filter statement of ISO/IEC 39075:2024,
   feature GQ08. Both spellings the standard defines are accepted, in every position the
   standard allows.
  </para>

  <para>
   openCypher has no <command>FILTER</command> statement. The equivalent there is a
   <literal>WHERE</literal> attached to <command>MATCH</command> or to
   <command>WITH</command>, and AgensGraph accepts both of those unchanged. The unrelated
   openCypher list function <literal>filter(x IN list WHERE predicate)</literal> is not
   accepted. Write a list comprehension instead, such as
   <literal>[x IN m.embedding WHERE x &gt; 0]</literal>.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

